From 68b65b83aaa78a6f2c1ab2247d0911016a51072e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 08:02:12 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] renderers/drm: Show the splash on stereo 3D outputs

plymouth.stereo=CONNECTOR:LAYOUT[:OPTION...][,...] names the outputs
that show two eyes and how. The HDMI 1.4b 3D structures (frame-packing,
sbs-full, sbs-half, tab) are carried by the mode: the renderer asks the
kernel for the stereo modes (DRM_CLIENT_CAP_STEREO_3D) and for their
aspect-ratio flags, picks mode=WxH@mHz the way the compositor picks it,
first in the connector's list with that size, refresh rate and 3D
structure, and draws the eyes into the layout drm_mode_set_crtcinfo
gives the mode (frame packing: the second eye at line vtotal). The
other layouts draw into the output's 2D mode: sbs-half-2d and tab-2d
for screens without 3D signalling, red/cyan anaglyph with Eric Dubois's
matrices in linear light (anaglyph-modern, anaglyph-crt), rows, columns
and checkerboard, sequential (one eye per refresh, page flipped), and
the panels of a stereo pair (left, right, mirrored with hflip or vflip,
the iZ3D back and front panels with formula=N and encoded). swap shows
each eye's picture to the other eye; card=cardN names the device when
two GPUs have a connector of the same name.

A theme that draws one picture shows it in both eyes. On a 2D output of
a stereo boot a stereo theme shows its left eye. A stereo boot skips
simpledrm and waits for the GPU's driver, which has the modes and the
outputs it names. Without plymouth.stereo= nothing changes.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
